A court is only as honest as it is expensive to fake.

while (verdict_pending) {
  validator.locks(GEN);
  judge(case);
  split(fee, 90, 10);
}

@GenLayer anyone can spin up a thousand AI judges, so if ruling cost nothing, one person could fill the court with their own. Locking GEN before a vote changes that math. A validator needs its own 42,000 GEN, its own infrastructure, and a place in the top 1,543, and its weight grows with the square root of stake, so doubling a stake adds about 41% weight, not double weight.

Users pay in GEN for execution, escrowed up front, with unused slots refunded. From each fee, 90% goes to validators on the majority side and 10% to developers of the contract. DeepThought takes 10% of the validators share, so it grows only when the network is actually used.

Losing a vote costs a validator its fee. Proven fraud is priced differently: leaders are slashed 5% and other signers 1%. An honest mistake and cheating do not cost the same, and that is the point of the lock.

If an appeal fails, the next jury is larger, so taking over an outcome gets harder the more you try. That is why a stake is not a ticket. It is the cost of being wrong.
